<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Maedi</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 80 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Thracian tribe of the middle Strymon valley that raided Macedon for centuries and fought Philip II, Alexander and Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Maedi lived along the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> (Struma) in modern south-western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from at least the 5th century BCE, as independent Thracian highlanders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known mainly from Greek and Roman accounts of war, the Maedi were a tribal people of hill country, often raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>. In 340 BCE the young Alexander, regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a>, defeated them and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandropolis-thrace">Alexandroupolis</a> in their land. Some scholars read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> as saying that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a> was a Maedian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> became a Roman province in 146 BCE, the Maedi raided it repeatedly; Roman commanders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> fought them in the 80s BCE. They were later absorbed into the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/maedi.jpg" alt="Maedi" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: Slovenski Volk at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PreMacedon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 80 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Thracian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thracian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maedians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-290&amp;focus=maedi" aria-label="Maedi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5285 670 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Maedi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5605 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5605 926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Bulgaria. <a href="/atlas?year=-290&amp;focus=maedi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

